<article class="env-lemma" id="009R">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/009R">Lemma <span data-tag="009R">6.30.10</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space. Let $\mathcal{B}$ be a basis for the topology on $X$. Let $(\mathcal{C}, F)$ be a type of algebraic structure. Denote $\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{B}, \mathcal{C})$ the category of sheaves with values in $\mathcal{C}$ on $\mathcal{B}$. There is an equivalence of categories </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (X, \mathcal{C}) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{B}, \mathcal{C})  \]
</div>
  <p> which assigns to a sheaf on $X$ its restriction to the members of $\mathcal{B}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The inverse functor in given in Lemma <a href="/tag/009Q" data-tag="009Q">6.30.9</a> above. Checking the obvious functorialities is left to the reader. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>